/incidencematrixmodule\/incidencematrix","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The unoriented incidence matrix of a hypergraph: an n × m matrix of bits with cell (v, p) set"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"when vertex "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"v"},{"type":"text","text":" is a member of column "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"p"},{"type":"text","text":". The vertices are "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"0..<vertexCount"},{"type":"text","text":" and the columns,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the hyperedges, are at positions "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"0..<hyperedgeCount"},{"type":"text","text":". It conforms to "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Hypergraph"},{"type":"text","text":", so the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Hypergraphs"},{"type":"text","text":" algorithms run on it unchanged; it is not a "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Graph"},{"type":"text","text":" even when built from one,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"because a cell edit can give a column any number of bits."}],"navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"IncidenceMatrix"}],"type":"topic","title":"IncidenceMatrix"},"doc://GraphProtocols/documentation/GraphProtocols/DirectedHypergraph":{"fragments":[{"text":"protocol","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"DirectedHypergraph","kind":"identifier"}],"title":"DirectedHypergraph","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A directed hypergraph: a finite set of vertices and a finite collection of hyperedges, each an"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"ordered pair of vertex sets, its sources (tail) and its targets (head). Gallo, Longo, Pallottino"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"and Nguyen, “Directed hypergraphs and applications”, "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"Discrete Applied Mathematics","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"42(2–3):177–201, 1993, define a hyperarc as “an ordered pair … of (possibly empty) disjoint"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"subsets”; here either side may be empty and a vertex may be on both, the hypergraph form of a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"self-loop (XGI’s "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"DiHypergraph"},{"type":"text","text":" allows both)."}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/DirectedHypergraph","url":"\/documentation\/graphprotocols\/directedhypergraph","kind":"symbol","type":"topic","role":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"DirectedHypergraph","kind":"identifier"}]}}}